/**
\page org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2 The Volume Visualization v2 View

\imageMacro{volume_visualization_v2-dox.svg,"Icon of the Volume Visualization v2 View.",2.00}

\tableofcontents

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_overview Overview

Volume visualization draws a 3D image as a solid body in the 3D render window.
Instead of showing one slice at a time, it sends a ray through the volume for
every pixel and accumulates colour and opacity from every voxel that ray meets.
Bone, vessels or skin can therefore be seen at once, in depth, without cutting
the data into planes first.

What decides whether you see bone, skin or fog is the <b>transfer function</b>:
curves that map each grey value to a colour and to an opacity. Almost everything
in this view is either a way of choosing a transfer function or a rendering
option applied on top of one.

\imageMacro{QmitkVolumeVisualizationV2_Overview.png,"The Volume Visualization v2 view.",10.00}

\note Volume rendering is done on the graphics card and has no software
fallback. On a machine whose GPU cannot run the ray caster, the 3D window stays
empty and the preset previews described below are not drawn either. Volume
rendering also needs a large amount of memory; very large images may not fit.

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_image Image

The selector at the top lists the 3D and 4D images in the Data Manager. Pick
one and switch <b>Volume rendering</b> on; the button names the state it is
currently in. Nothing below the selector responds while it is off, because
there is no volume to configure yet.

Switching it on does more than show the volume: a node that carries no transfer
function is given one from the preset catalogue, and one that names no lighting
model is given the first of those. Switching on therefore produces a visible
volume rather than an empty 3D window, and you adjust from there.

Which preset that is follows from the image. One whose DICOM metadata reports MR
starts from <tt>MR-Default</tt>; everything else starts from <tt>CT-AAA</tt>,
including an image carrying no such metadata - a NIfTI or an NRRD from outside
MITK usually carries none. Seeing a CT preset on an MR loaded from one of those
formats is therefore expected; pick an <tt>MR-</tt> preset from the grid.

For a 4D image the volume follows the current time step, so stepping through
time in the image navigator steps the volume with it.

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf Transfer function

\subsection org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_presets Presets

<b>Select a preset</b> folds out a grid of presets, each shown as a small
rendering of the image you currently have selected. The preview is what applying
that preset will look like on your data, not a generic illustration, which is
why the presets are chosen by picture rather than by name. They are drawn when
the grid opens, one after another, so they appear progressively.

\imageMacro{QmitkVolumeVisualizationV2_Presets.png,"Each preset is shown as a rendering of the selected image.",12.00}

The catalogue is the Hounsfield-based preset library shared across medical
viewers, plus a few general-purpose colour maps. The <tt>CT-</tt> presets assume
Hounsfield units, so which tissue a preset picks out transfers between CT images
unchanged. How solid that tissue looks does not: opacity accumulates over the
distance a ray travels in millimetres, so the same preset comes out denser on a
coarsely sampled image than on a finely sampled one. <b>Opacity height</b> is
the slider that compensates. The <tt>MR-</tt> presets cannot assume even the
units, because MR intensities are not calibrated to a fixed scale, so expect to
adjust those per image.

While the grid is folded away its header names the current choice, or reads
<em>Preset: Custom</em> for a curve that no preset describes.

\subsection org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_canvas Reading the curve

Below the preset grid, one canvas shows three things at once: the histogram of
the image as a backdrop, the opacity curve drawn over it, and the colours the
transfer function assigns as a gradient beneath. Together they say which grey
values the current function makes visible and in what colour, against how much
of that value actually occurs in the image.

The canvas is a display, not an editor. Clicking it does nothing; the curve is
moved as a whole with the sliders described next. Editing point by point is
possible, in the separate mode described in
\ref org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_custom.

\imageMacro{QmitkVolumeVisualizationV2_Canvas.png,"Histogram backdrop with the opacity curve and the colour gradient.",10.00}

\subsection org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_adjust Adjusting a preset

A preset lands where its author put it, which is rarely exactly where your image
wants it. <b>Adjust preset</b> offers four sliders that move it without
redrawing it:

<ul>
<li> <b>Opacity shift</b> slides the opacity curve along the grey-value axis,
which is what you reach for when a preset shows too much or too little tissue.
It travels half the image's value range in either direction.
<li> <b>Opacity height</b> raises or lowers the whole curve, making the volume
more or less solid without changing which values are visible at all.
<li> <b>Color shift</b> moves the centre of the range the preset's colours are
spread over.
<li> <b>Color width</b> sets how wide that range is. Narrowing it concentrates
the colours into a smaller band of grey values; widening it spreads them out.
</ul>

<b>Reset sliders</b> returns all four to neutral. That restores the curve they
are measured from, which is the untouched preset, or the curve a file supplied
if the function came from one. It does not reload a preset.

What is remembered is the preset together with the four offsets, rather than
only the resulting curve, so selecting the image again later brings the sliders
back to the positions you left them in.

\subsection org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_custom Custom transfer functions

Under the preset grid, <b>Create custom...</b> and <b>Load from file...</b> lead
away from presets altogether. <b>Load from file...</b> applies a transfer
function saved to a JSON file earlier, straight away.

<b>Create custom...</b> hands the whole panel over to a point-by-point editor.
The image selector and the lighting section are put away for the duration and
the panel returns to its top, so what you are left looking at is a page that
names itself, names the image it is editing, and carries a way back at its top
left. It holds one canvas per curve: grey value to opacity, and grey value to
colour.

In both canvases:

<ul>
<li> Left-click adds a point.
<li> Left-click and drag moves an existing point; the arrow keys move the
selected one.
<li> Right-click, or the DELETE key, removes a point.
<li> Double-clicking a point in the colour canvas opens a colour picker.
<li> Between points the curves are interpolated linearly.
</ul>

The page brings three buttons, and the arrow at its top left repeats one of
them. <b>Keep curve</b> keeps the curve you authored and goes back.
<b>Discard</b> restores the one that stood before you started, and so does the
top-left arrow, so that a way out is on screen whatever the page is scrolled to;
neither asks first. <b>Save to file...</b> writes the current function to a JSON
file in the same format the built-in catalogue uses, so your own functions can
be kept and shared and read back with <b>Load from file...</b>.

\note A curve that has been authored or loaded is no longer described by any
preset name, so the name is dropped and the adjust sliders start measuring from
the new curve. A saved file records the blend mode the curve was authored for
alongside it, and <b>Load</b> restores both.

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_blendmode Blend mode

The blend mode is the rule for combining the samples along one viewing ray into
one pixel:

<ul>
<li> <b>Composite (3D)</b> accumulates colour and opacity front to back, so
nearer tissue hides what lies behind it. It is the only mode that produces a
three-dimensional image, and the only one lighting and shading reach.
<li> <b>Maximum intensity (MIP)</b> keeps the brightest sample along each ray.
Depth is lost, but anything dense stays visible however much tissue surrounds
it, which is what makes contrast-filled vessels and tracer uptake readable.
<li> <b>Minimum intensity (MinIP)</b> keeps the darkest sample instead, so air
stands out against tissue - airways, emphysema, bowel gas.
<li> <b>Average intensity</b> averages the samples along each ray, which reads
like a projection radiograph.
<li> <b>Additive intensity</b> sums them. Like the average but unbounded, so it
saturates towards white where the volume is deep.
</ul>

Average and additive ignore the colour curve and produce greyscale; the other
three use it.

The mode is not a setting of its own but part of a transfer function. The
projection modes flatten each ray to a single value before the transfer
function sees it, so a curve drawn to classify tissue and one drawn as a window
are not interchangeable between them: a preset that hides everything but bone
leaves a maximum-intensity projection almost nothing to find. Applying a preset
therefore also applies the mode it was authored for, and the preset previews
are drawn in that mode. Whenever the result is anything other than Composite,
the view names it in a line under <b>Volume rendering</b>.

Almost every preset in the catalogue is authored for Composite. <tt>CT-MIP</tt>
and <tt>MR-MIP</tt> are the two that are not, and they bring curves that ramp
across the whole value range instead of isolating a tissue, which is what a
projection needs. To pick a mode yourself, use <b>Blend mode</b> at the top of
the <b>Create custom...</b> page, where you are authoring the curve that goes
with it.

VTK also defines an isosurface and a slice mode, which are deliberately absent
here. Each needs input that nothing in MITK supplies - iso-values, or a cutting
plane - and without it the ray caster draws nothing at all rather than falling
back, so offering either would only ever produce an empty 3D window.

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_lighting Lighting and shading

Shading gives the volume the shape cues a flat colour cannot: where a surface
turns away from the light, where two structures touch, how deep a recess goes.

It is always on for a volume rendered here. The <b>Lighting model</b> picks how
the volume is lit, and the two models are not weaker and stronger versions of
the same effect - they take different paths through the renderer:

<ul>
<li> <b>Headlight</b> puts a single light at the camera. Nothing casts a
visible shadow, because the light reaches exactly what you can see. In exchange
it is the only model in which the <b>Ambient</b> slider carries the tissue's own
colour, so ambient light fills the dark areas instead of laying flat grey over
the picture.
<li> <b>Key light</b> puts the main light off to one side and adds a dimmer one
opposite it, then traces short secondary rays through the volume so that
contacts and recesses darken. The rays are kept short deliberately: long ones
let one structure cast a shadow onto another, which describes the lighting
rather than the anatomy. This model costs more per still frame, switches its
shadows off while you rotate, and also relights any surface sharing the 3D
window for as long as this view is open.
</ul>

<b>Ambient</b>, <b>Diffuse</b>, <b>Specular</b> and <b>Specular power</b>
control how the material responds to that light. Choosing a model sets all four
to a sensible starting point; moving them afterwards does not leave the model,
and <b>Reset sliders</b> puts them back to the values the current model
prescribes. Ambient is deliberately zero under Key light: with more than one
light in the scene the renderer adds ambient as a flat grey wash, so the fill
light does that job instead.

\note This whole section applies to the Composite blend mode only, and is
disabled otherwise, with the reason on its header. The projection modes reduce
each ray to a single value and light nothing, so lighting there is inert rather
than merely subtle.

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_appearance Colour and opacity from the Data Manager

A volume's appearance comes from its transfer function, not from the node's
generic appearance properties.

The Data Manager's Opacity slider fades the image in the 2D windows, and in the
3D window it fades the slice planes the image is textured onto - but not the
volume itself. Use the opacity part of the transfer function for that.

The Color action likewise has no effect on a grey image, whose colour the
transfer function supplies. On a binary image it does apply: there the volume is
drawn in that single colour, and the transfer function controls are disabled,
as the view says when you select such an image.
*/
